

## 1. Summary

Monitoring a Linux system means checking how well its hardware and software work. It helps you **track performance, find resource-heavy processes and detect issues early**. It involves knowing your CPU, memory, disks and processes, watching them in real time, and understanding the hardware configuration and operating environment.

---

## 2. Checking system information

| Command | Definition | Details |
|---------|------------|---------|
| `hostnamectl` | Shows system identity in a tidy block. | Hostname, machine ID, chassis, OS, kernel, virtualization. Confirms the distro and whether you are in a VM or container. |
| `lscpu` | Lists CPU information. | Model name, sockets, cores, threads per core, flags (SSE/AVX). Useful to estimate parallel capacity. |
| `lsblk` | Lists block devices as a tree. | Disks, partitions, LVM layers, sizes, mount points. Options: `--fs`, `-o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,MOUNTPOINT`. |
| `free -h` | Shows RAM and swap usage in human-readable units. | Total, used, free and available memory. |
| `df -h` | Shows disk space used and available per mounted filesystem. | Check `Use%` for nearly full volumes; add `-T` for filesystem types. |

---

## 3. Real-time performance monitoring

| Command | Definition | Details |
|---------|------------|---------|
| `top` | Live process viewer. | CPU/memory per process and load averages. Sort with Shift+P (CPU) or Shift+M (memory). |
| `htop` | Colorful, interactive version of `top`. | Scrolling, tree view, easy sort/filter, per-core usage, quick process kill. May need installation. |
| `iostat -xz 1` | Shows how busy your disks are. | `-x` extended stats, `-z` hides idle devices, `1` refreshes every second. High `%util` or `avgqu-sz` with rising `await` means overloaded disks. |
| `iftop` | Real-time network usage per connection. | Shows who sends/receives the most data; helps find noisy hosts. |

---

## 4. Hardware and devices

| Command | Definition | Details |
|---------|------------|---------|
| `lspci` | Lists PCI devices (graphics cards, network adapters...). | Confirms hardware detected by the kernel; useful for driver troubleshooting. |
| `lsusb` | Lists connected USB devices. | Vendor/product IDs; check recognition before looking at `dmesg` or drivers. |
| `sudo dmidecode -t system` | Shows manufacturer, model, serial, BIOS/UEFI version. | Reads DMI/SMBIOS data. Other types: `-t bios`, `-t memory`. |

---

## 5. Logs and system events

| Command | Definition | Details |
|---------|------------|---------|
| `journalctl -xe` | Shows recent system errors and warnings. | Extra context on errors and critical events; a good first stop after a failure. |
| `journalctl -u servicename` | Shows logs of one service. | Add `--since` / `--until` for time ranges, `-f` to follow live. |
| `dmesg -T` | Shows kernel messages with readable timestamps. | Hardware detection, driver issues, crash traces. |

---

## 6. Services and startup

| Command | Definition | Details |
|---------|------------|---------|
| `systemctl status service-name` | Shows if a service is running. | Status, last logs, main PID, recent failures. |
| `systemctl enable --now service-name` | Enables a service at boot and starts it now. | Use after installation or configuration. |
| `systemctl --failed` | Lists services that failed to start. | A good starting point to triage after boot. |

---

## 7. Troubleshooting scenarios

| Problem | Command |
|---------|---------|
| High CPU usage | `top` or `htop` |
| Low available memory | `free -h` |
| Disk almost full | `df -h` |
| Find large disks/partitions | `lsblk` |
| Service not starting | `systemctl status service-name` |
| Check service logs | `journalctl -u service-name` |
| View kernel errors | `dmesg -T` |
| Check failed services | `systemctl --failed` |

---

## 8. Basic security and user checks

| Command | Definition | Details |
|---------|------------|---------|
| `id` | Shows the current user ID and groups. | UID, primary and supplementary groups; verify membership in `sudo` or `docker`. |
| `sudo -l` | Lists commands the user can run with sudo. | Based on sudoers; useful for least-privilege audits. |
| `last -n 10` | Shows the last 10 logins. | Usernames, times, sources; helps spot suspicious logins. |

